Why the run-time ABI's names are here
-------------------------------------

On a bare-metal Arm target, such as clang's armv4t-none-eabi, clang calls
the Arm run-time ABI's __aeabi_memcpy, __aeabi_memmove, __aeabi_memset and
__aeabi_memclr families in place of the standard names. src/ calls
__aeabi_memclr4 or __aeabi_memclr8 at every level there. GCC calls the
standard names. musl's two files give all twelve, as loops of one byte at
a time.


Functions in C instead
----------------------

memset and memcmp come from libterae's own C, in src/port/generic, which
port_memset.c and port_memcmp.c include. Nothing in it is taken from
elsewhere. Armv4T interworks only through BX, so a routine that returns by
loading pc, with LDM, POP or MOV, returns in ARM state to a Thumb caller and
fails there. The candidates and why each was set aside:

memset   NetBSD's memset_arm.S and memset_naive.S (BSD-2-Clause) are
         written in the divided syntax that clang's assembler doesn't
         take. NetBSD's memset.S and OpenBSD's carry an advertising
         clause (Wasabi Systems, Mark Brinicombe), which isn't
         compatible with the GPL. Arm Optimized Routines' memset.S is
         Thumb-2, which Armv4T and Armv5TE lack. newlib's
         aeabi_memset-arm.S takes the run-time ABI's order of
         arguments, not memset's. NuttX's armv7-m arch_memset.S is
         Thumb-2, and Android's ARM original returns by loading pc.

memcmp   OpenBSD's memcmp.S (BSD-3-Clause, ARM Ltd) returns with
         MOV pc, lr. Android's generic memcmp.S uses PLD, which needs
         Armv5TE, and assumes little-endian order. NetBSD's memcmp.S
         carries Wasabi Systems' advertising clause. newlib, picolibc,
         NuttX and Arm Optimized Routines have none for 32-bit Arm.

memmove  Is provided, but only as a loop that copies one byte at a time.
         NetBSD's memmove.S (BSD-2-Clause), which handles either byte
         order, returns with POP {r0, pc}, and hands copies that don't
         overlap to NetBSD's own memcpy.


